      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lupton began as a Quaker settlement called Lane Heights, with a post office named Lane opened in 1881.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/lupton-michigan-ghost-town/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A Detroit, Bay City and Alpena Railroad depot opened in January 1893, linking Lupton to Michigan’s growing rail network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lupton%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the 1920s, Lupton’s timber decline gave way to a secret Purple Gang hideout, complete with underground tunnels and a notorious Graceland Ballroom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/lupton-michigan-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Great Depression dealt Lupton its final blow, driving 34% unemployment and prompting a mass exodus that left many buildings abandoned.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/lupton-michigan-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1995, Lupton’s historic depot was honored as a Michigan State Historic Site, preserving its legacy for future generations.",
